    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§6802 & 6807, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4313\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\ncreating the topical medication safety and efficacy act\n\nPURPOSE: This bill gives physicians the limited and specific ability\nto dispense topical pharmaceuticals where such distribution would\nenhance the safety or efficacy of the medication for that patient.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS: Section one contains a short title for the act.\n\nSection two contains legislative findings and Intent. Section three\namends section 6802 of the education law to provide a definition of a\ntopical pharmaceutical for purposes of the act. Section four amends\nsubdivision 2 of section 6807 of the education law and adds a new\nsubparagraph 10 to allow physicians to dispense certain topical\npharmaceuticals in excess of the seventy-two hour supply limit\ngenerally imposed by subdivision 2 of Section 6807. Section five\nestablishes the effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW: Currently, physicians are limited to dispensing a 72\nhour supply of prescription drugs unless they meet one of the\nexemptions provided in section 6807(2) of the education law.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: Modern pharmaceutical research has expanded the ability\nof physicians to heal their patients, but these new discoveries\nrequire not just new chemical compounds, but also occasionally require\nnew methods of administering treatments and distributing medicines to\npatients. For some topically applied medications, the safety and\nefficacy of the treatments are enhanced when patients can obtain their\nmedicines directly from their physicians and immediately learn correct\napplication techniques under the doctor's supervision.\n\nThis is especially true in the case of medications used to treat skin\ndiscoloration and certain eye ailments. Due to the sensitivity of the\nskin and eyes, patients often require additional guidance from\nphysicians in the proper administration of treatments, and this method\nof instruction coupled with dispensing by physicians currently helps\nmillions of patients around the country.\n\nUnfortunately, New York State law has failed to similarly keep pace\nwith medical developments, and New York is one of only three states in\nthe nation that prohibits the dispensing of medications by physicians.\nThis has resulted in certain medications not being as accessible to\nNew York patients as they are in other states, thus keeping some\npatients away from desired treatments and driving others out of state\nto obtain treatment.\n\nTo address this, this bill would allow consumers to access, in a\ndoctor's office setting, certain identified pharmaceuticals that are\ndispensed only to change physical appearance if a doctor meets\nspecified criteria.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
